//! THE SHARED STATS REGION — the ONE storage the trap columns live in, and the
//! page a process maps to read them (sawos design 37, M5 unit 7; design 25
//! seed 1).
//!
//! **THE STORAGE MOVED; THE COUNTING SITES DID NOT.** The three columns used to
//! be fields of `ProcessSlot`. They are rows of this region now, and
//! `ProcessOp.Stats` reads them from here — so the v1 op answers identical
//! numbers out of the new home, and there is no mirror to keep in step. That is
//! the whole of the relocation: one storage, two doors.
//!
//! **WHERE THIS SITS IN THE MODULE ORDER, and why it is a module at all.** It
//! names `kcore.mem` (the alignment arithmetic), `kcore.diag` (the kernel-bug
//! report) and the HAL, and it is named BY the trap path, the stats op, the
//! process reset and the map op. Everything that writes a column is above it and
//! nothing it calls can reach back — the same test `mem` and `diag` pass, which
//! is why it belongs down beside them rather than inside `pslot` (which holds
//! records and does no work) or inside `dispatch` (which is at the top and would
//! make the loader's boot-time publish an upward reach).
//!
//! # THE CAPACITY DECISION, RECORDED HERE BECAUSE THIS IS WHERE IT IS SPENT
//!
//! Design 37 asked for the region's row capacity to be decided honestly, and
//! named two candidates: the compiled floor's rows in a static page with
//! rows-beyond-floor as a follow-on, or the region sized for
//! `PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS`-scale counts up front.
//!
//! **THE FIRST IS NOT AVAILABLE, and finding out why is what settled it.** Once
//! the columns RELOCATE, a process slot with no row has nowhere to count — so a
//! floor-sized region would silently stop counting the donated slots M5 unit 6b
//! exists to create. "Follow-on" would mean shipping a kernel that miscounts.
//!
//! **THE SECOND IS NOT MERELY BIGGER, IT IS EXACT.** `alloc_process` scans to
//! `process_slots_usable()` = `min(PROCESSES.capacity(), hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS)`
//! (design 36's wall), so `PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` is a CEILING on the slot index the
//! machine can ever hand out — donation raises the capacity and cannot raise
//! that. A region with `PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS` rows therefore has a row for every
//! process that can ever exist on this build, and the follow-on the brief was
//! willing to accept is not owed at all.
//!
//! The cost is `.bss` and nothing else (design 149: an all-zero static is
//! zerofill in both profiles, so the image carries none of it) — see the
//! measurements in design 37's As-built.

import hal

import sosabi.{StatsRow, StatsRegionHeader, STATS_REGION_VERSION,
               PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS, PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT,
               PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT, PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT}

import kcore.mem.{align_up}
import kcore.diag.{fatal_kernel}

// =============================================================================
// The shape, in doublewords
// =============================================================================
//
// THE BACKING IS RAW DOUBLEWORDS AND THE ABI IS TYPED STRUCTS, and the two are
// pinned to each other by the asserts below rather than by agreement. That split
// is deliberate: a READER overlays `sosabi`'s `StatsRegionHeader` / `StatsRow`
// and reads by field name (the blessed typed-view idiom), while the WRITER is
// the trap path, which must touch ONE doubleword per trap. A typed
// read-modify-write of a whole row would be three loads and three stores where
// one of each will do, on the hottest path in the kernel, to serve a spelling
// preference. So the write side indexes by the slot constants `sosabi` already
// publishes for the copy-out record — the same names, describing the same
// layout, which is what keeps this from being magic-number arithmetic.

/// Bytes in a doubleword. Named because it appears in the offset arithmetic
/// below, and asserted rather than assumed.
static DOUBLEWORD_BYTES: Int = 8

static_assert(sizeof<UInt64>() == DOUBLEWORD_BYTES,
              "a doubleword must be eight bytes for the region arithmetic")

/// Doublewords in the published header.
static HEADER_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = 4

static_assert(sizeof<StatsRegionHeader>() == HEADER_DOUBLEWORDS * DOUBLEWORD_BYTES,
              "the header's doubleword count must match the struct sosabi publishes")

/// Where each header field sits, in doublewords from the region's base. The
/// order is `StatsRegionHeader`'s declaration order, which IS its layout.
static HEADER_VERSION_SLOT: Int = 0
static HEADER_ROWS_SLOT: Int = 1
static HEADER_ROW_BYTES_SLOT: Int = 2
static HEADER_ROWS_OFFSET_SLOT: Int = 3

/// Doublewords in one row — `sosabi`'s own number, not a second one.
static ROW_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = PROCESS_STATS_RECORD_DOUBLEWORDS

static_assert(sizeof<StatsRow>() == ROW_DOUBLEWORDS * DOUBLEWORD_BYTES,
              "a row's doubleword count must match the struct sosabi publishes")

/// **HOW MANY ROWS THE REGION PUBLISHES** — one per process slot this build can
/// ever hand out. See the capacity argument in the module header.
public(package) static STATS_ROWS: Int = hal.PROT_DOMAIN_SLOTS

/// The alignment and granularity the region is PLACED and SIZED at when it is
/// mapped, as a compile-time bound.
///
/// **IT IS THE COARSEST `PROT_GRAIN` ANY PROFILE IN THIS TREE PUBLISHES, and it
/// is a constant here because a STORAGE SIZE has to fold.** The runtime
/// arithmetic below aligns to `hal.PROT_GRAIN` — whatever this build's own
/// granularity is — so the region a caller maps is exactly as tight as its
/// hardware allows. What this constant sizes is the SLACK the static has to
/// carry so that an aligned base exists inside it at all, and a bound is all it
/// has to be: the assert below is what keeps it true as profiles are added.
static REGION_ALIGN: Int = 4096

static_assert(hal.PROT_GRAIN <= 4096,
              "the stats region's compiled slack must cover this profile's protection grain")

static_assert((REGION_ALIGN % DOUBLEWORD_BYTES) == 0,
              "the region's alignment must be a whole number of doublewords")

/// The published region's own size — header plus every row.
static LIVE_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = HEADER_DOUBLEWORDS + STATS_ROWS * ROW_DOUBLEWORDS
static LIVE_BYTES: Int = LIVE_DOUBLEWORDS * DOUBLEWORD_BYTES

/// The static's size: the mapped span rounded up to the coarsest grain, plus one
/// more grain so an aligned base is reachable wherever the linker lands the
/// array. Both halves are the reason a caller never sees a byte the kernel did
/// not mean to publish — the tail of the last granule is the region's OWN
/// storage, so rounding the span up leaks nothing.
/// (Written on one line because a `static` initializer may not wrap after the
/// `=` — SL-22, filed by design 32 and still open.)
static STORAGE_BYTES: Int = ((LIVE_BYTES + REGION_ALIGN - 1) / REGION_ALIGN) * REGION_ALIGN + REGION_ALIGN
static STORAGE_DOUBLEWORDS: Int = STORAGE_BYTES / DOUBLEWORD_BYTES

/// **THE REGION ITSELF.** An `unsafe static var` (design 149) rather than a
/// `Once` or an `Atomic`, on the arena's terms exactly: it is compound state
/// mutated throughout, its consistency spans words, and spec §7's uniprocessor
/// argument plus interrupts-masked-in-the-kernel is what serializes it.
///
/// ALL-ZERO, so it is zerofill in both profiles and costs the image nothing
/// (design 149). The header is written at boot by `stats_publish` rather than
/// declared, which is what keeps that true — a non-zero initializer would move
/// the whole region out of `.bss`.
unsafe static var STATS_STORAGE: [UInt64; STORAGE_DOUBLEWORDS] = [0; STORAGE_DOUBLEWORDS]

// =============================================================================
// Where the published region starts
// =============================================================================

/// The doubleword index within `STATS_STORAGE` at which the published region
/// begins — the alignment slack, measured rather than assumed.
///
/// The array is `[UInt64; N]`, so its address is already doubleword-aligned,
/// and a `PROT_GRAIN` is a power of two — so the distance to the next boundary
/// is a whole number of doublewords whichever side of eight the granularity
/// falls on. A grain FINER than a doubleword is already satisfied and the
/// distance is zero; a coarser one is a multiple of eight.
func stats_first() unsafe -> Int {
    let raw = ((&var STATS_STORAGE) as UnsafePointer<UInt64>) as UInt
    let aligned = align_up(raw, hal.PROT_GRAIN)
    ((aligned - raw) / (DOUBLEWORD_BYTES as UInt)) as Int
}

/// **WHERE THE REGION IS TO THE KERNEL** — the address the kernel's own loads
/// and stores use. Aligned to this profile's real protection granularity.
func stats_region_base() unsafe -> UInt {
    align_up(((&var STATS_STORAGE) as UnsafePointer<UInt64>) as UInt, hal.PROT_GRAIN)
}

/// **WHERE THE REGION IS TO THE HARDWARE** — the PHYSICAL base a protection row
/// has to name, which is what the map op grants.
///
/// **THE TWO ARE NOT THE SAME ADDRESS ON EVERY TIER, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE
/// REASON THIS FUNCTION EXISTS** (sawos design 29's linear-map seam). A kernel
/// static is reached by the kernel through whatever address the kernel runs at;
/// a grant row's output address is a FRAME. On a tier whose kernel lives in the
/// linear map those differ by a fixed offset, and handing the kernel's own
/// address to a protection row produces a descriptor whose output address is not
/// memory — which the hardware reports as an ADDRESS SIZE fault on the first
/// read, not as a missing mapping. `hal.virt_to_phys` is identity where the two
/// coincide, so this is one spelling on every profile rather than a branch.
public(package) func stats_region_phys() unsafe -> UInt {
    hal.virt_to_phys(stats_region_base())
}

/// **HOW MUCH OF IT IS GRANTED** — the published size rounded up to this
/// profile's granularity, because a protection row is granular and a row that
/// stopped mid-granule is not expressible.
public(package) func stats_region_len() -> UInt {
    align_up(LIVE_BYTES as UInt, hal.PROT_GRAIN)
}

// =============================================================================
// The columns
// =============================================================================

/// Which doubleword of the storage a row's column sits at.
func column_at(first: Int, row: Int, column: Int) -> Int {
    first + HEADER_DOUBLEWORDS + row * ROW_DOUBLEWORDS + column
}

/// **IS THERE A ROW FOR THIS SLOT?** Structurally always yes — see the module
/// header's capacity argument — so a `false` here is the kernel disagreeing with
/// its own wall, which is a kernel bug and is reported as one rather than
/// absorbed. `fatal_kernel` stops the machine and is not declared `-> Never`
/// (design 6's finding 4), so every caller exits its own scope after it.
func row_ok(row: Int) -> Bool {
    if row < 0 || row >= STATS_ROWS {
        return false
    }
    true
}

/// Add one to a column of `row`. The trap path's whole contact with the region.
///
/// ONE LOAD AND ONE STORE, which is why the write side does not go through a
/// typed overlay (see the section header above).
public(package) func stats_bump(row: Int, column: Int) unsafe {
    if not row_ok(row) {
        fatal_kernel("a trap was charged to a process slot with no stats row",
                     row as UInt)
        return
    }
    let at = column_at(stats_first(), row, column)
    STATS_STORAGE[at] = STATS_STORAGE[at] + 1
}

/// Read `row`'s three columns out of the region — what `ProcessOp.Stats`
/// answers with, from the one storage rather than from a copy of it.
public(package) func stats_read(row: Int) unsafe -> StatsRow {
    if not row_ok(row) {
        fatal_kernel("stats were asked for a process slot with no row", row as UInt)
        return StatsRow(syscalls: 0, interrupts: 0, faults: 0)
    }
    let first = stats_first()
    StatsRow(syscalls: STATS_STORAGE[column_at(first, row, PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT)],
             interrupts: STATS_STORAGE[column_at(first, row, PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT)],
             faults: STATS_STORAGE[column_at(first, row, PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT)])
}

/// Zero `row`'s columns — what a process create does, so a reused slot never
/// inherits its predecessor's traffic (the rule `sosabi`'s record block states).
public(package) func stats_clear(row: Int) unsafe {
    if not row_ok(row) {
        fatal_kernel("stats were cleared for a process slot with no row", row as UInt)
        return
    }
    let first = stats_first()
    STATS_STORAGE[column_at(first, row, PROCESS_STATS_SYSCALLS_SLOT)] = 0
    STATS_STORAGE[column_at(first, row, PROCESS_STATS_INTERRUPTS_SLOT)] = 0
    STATS_STORAGE[column_at(first, row, PROCESS_STATS_FAULTS_SLOT)] = 0
}

// =============================================================================
// Publishing
// =============================================================================

/// **WRITE THE HEADER**, once, before anything can count.
///
/// It is a boot-time write rather than a static initializer because an all-zero
/// static is zerofill and a non-zero one is not: declaring the version would
/// move the whole region — every row of it — out of `.bss` and into the image
/// (design 149's rule, and design 36's `.bss` -> `.data` finding is the same
/// trap seen from the other side). Four stores at boot buy that back.
public(package) func stats_publish() unsafe {
    let first = stats_first()
    STATS_STORAGE[first + HEADER_VERSION_SLOT] = STATS_REGION_VERSION
    STATS_STORAGE[first + HEADER_ROWS_SLOT] = STATS_ROWS as UInt64
    let row_bytes = ROW_DOUBLEWORDS * DOUBLEWORD_BYTES
    let rows_offset = HEADER_DOUBLEWORDS * DOUBLEWORD_BYTES
    STATS_STORAGE[first + HEADER_ROW_BYTES_SLOT] = row_bytes as UInt64
    STATS_STORAGE[first + HEADER_ROWS_OFFSET_SLOT] = rows_offset as UInt64
}
